; island.mx -- a rewrite over real C, in text mode. Stage 5's rules.
;
; The body this is used on is not written to fit a grammar: it is
; metaxis/cmd/mx.c, the tool's own front end, exactly as it is in the tree.
; Text mode is an island grammar by construction -- a rule fires where it
; matches and everything else is copied through -- so a file that declares
; two constructs can be pointed at a real file and leave the rest alone.
; That is what the survey in docs/prior-art.md §3.3 said was the strongest
; thing this tool lacked, and text mode turned out to have it already.
;
; The rewrite: every `fprintf(stderr, "mx: %s\n", X)` becomes `complain(X)`,
; and the rule that does it *contributes* the definition of `complain` to a
; collection -- once, however many times it fires -- which the rule for
; `usage` splices in front of that function. So the definition exists only
; if something calls it, and the file that names the landmark does not have
; to know what goes there. The landmark itself is still a workaround: the
; honest place is *before the first function*, and nothing here can say
; that without reading C.
;
; What text mode did not know on the day this was written, measured on this
; file and recorded in docs/COMPLETED.md's *The island rule, finished*: it
; had no tokens, so a word fired inside an identifier or a string; it had no
; brackets, so a hole over `f(x, g(y))` stopped at the first `)` and was
; right only when the template kept the hole last; and declaring C's
; comments as @comment removed them. All three are declarations now. The
; four @token classes make the scan move by tokens where they match, so a
; word fires only on a whole identifier, and a string, a character literal
; or a comment is passed over rather than searched. The three @bracket pairs
; make a hole stop only where they balance, so `e` below is the whole
; argument. The third rule is the rename that used to go wrong: `out` is the
; buffer the front end writes, and `outpath`, the `output` in the usage
; string and the `out` in the file's first comment are all left alone. The
; fourth puts text after its hole, which is right only because the hole is
; whole: without the brackets it wrote `(f = fopen(outpath, "wb") == 0))`,
; which compiles and means something else.
;
; These are code templates, and the first draft's were strings. In a string
; template `{` is a hole, so C's braces are `{{ }}`, and `\n` is a newline, so
; the C source's own `\n` is `\\n`; a code template's strings have the second
; rule and not the first, and `contribute` is a statement, which a string
; template cannot say at all.

@mode text

@token name    "[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*"
@token string  "\"([^\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*\""
@token char    "'([^'\\\\]|\\\\.)*'"
@token comment "/\\*([^*]|\\*+[^*/])*\\*+/"

@bracket "(" ")"
@bracket "[" "]"
@bracket "{" "}"

@syntax "fprintf(stderr, \"mx: %s\\n\", " e ")"
    => {
        contribute("defs", "static void complain(const char *e) { fprintf(stderr, \"mx: %s\\n\", e); }")
        emit "complain(" + e + ")"
    }

@syntax "static void usage(void)"
    => { emit splice("defs") + "\n\nstatic void usage(void)" }

@syntax "out" => "res"

@syntax "!(" x ")" => "({x}) == 0"
